  "body": "Conjecture: The function open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object, whose documented rule says, \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text, because a model places its final answer last and marks it: a reply that quotes its previous answer and then gives a corrected one is scored on the correction.\" I test two replies. Reply one is the text: '```json\\n{\\\"x\\\":1}\\n{\\\"y\\\":2}\\n```\\n{\\\"z\\\":3}'. Reply two is the text: '```json\\n{\\\"x\\\":1}\\n{\\\"w\\\":4}\\n```\\n{\\\"z\\\":3}'. For reply one, the rule requires the last object inside the code fence, which is {\\\"y\\\":2}. For reply two, the rule requires the last object inside the code fence, which is {\\\"w\\\":4}. The check, however, will return {\\\"z\\\":3} for both, because the fenced span is not a single valid JSON object, so the fenced pool yields no candidate, and the top-level pool's last candidate is {\\\"z\\\":3}. Thus the check gives the same answer on a pair the rule requires to tell apart. The observation that would show this conjecture false is if the check returns {\\\"y\\\":2} for reply one and {\\\"w\\\":4} for reply two, or if it returns any different pair of answers."
